@@ -0,0 +1,78 @@
+# Passkey UI and authentication service contract
+
+The sign-in, sign-up, and account settings screens use the external authentication service. This repository does **not** contain that service or a credential database. The UI does not simulate authentication or treat a browser credential response as verified. Deployment requires the service endpoints below; their availability on the existing service has not been verified.
+
+`PUBLIC_AUTH_API_URL` optionally overrides the existing API base URL (without a trailing slash). The default is `https://us-central1-web-devrel-apps.cloudfunctions.net/times_cdn/api`. Configure this at build time. Serve the UI on HTTPS, or localhost for development.
+
+Requests send JSON, `credentials: include`, and an `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header when the password/passkey verification endpoint returns a token. Cookie sessions are preferred; bearer tokens are kept in sessionStorage, never localStorage. For a cross-origin service, allow the specific UI origin, credentials, the methods below, and the Content-Type, Authorization, and X-Reauthentication-Token headers in CORS. Enforce origin/CSRF protection on the service. No secret belongs in a `PUBLIC_` variable.
+
+## Endpoints
+
+All successful JSON responses use Content-Type application/json. DELETE may return 204. Verification responses must be JSON even when using a cookie session (`{}` is sufficient for registration/authentication).
+
+| Method | Path | Request | Success response |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| POST | `/login` | `{ username, password }` | `{ token?, username? }`; establish session |
+| POST | `/register` | `{ username, password }` | Existing password registration response; UI prompts sign-in afterward |
+| GET | `/account` | Authenticated session | Account object below |
+| PUT | `/account` | `{ username, displayName }` and reauthentication header | Full updated account object |
+| POST | `/passkeys/register/options` | `{ username?, promotion }` and, in settings, reauthentication header | Native `PublicKeyCredentialCreationOptionsJSON` |
+| POST | `/passkeys/register/verify` | `{ credential, username? }` and, in settings, reauthentication header | `{ token?, username? }`; persist credential and establish/retain session |
+| POST | `/passkeys/authenticate/options` | `{}` | Native `PublicKeyCredentialRequestOptionsJSON` |
+| POST | `/passkeys/authenticate/verify` | `{ credential }` | `{ token?, username? }`; establish session |
+| PUT | `/passkeys/:id` | `{ name }` and reauthentication header | JSON object |
+| DELETE | `/passkeys/:id` | Reauthentication header | JSON object or 204 |
+| POST | `/passkeys/reauthenticate/options` | `{ action }`, authenticated session | Account-constrained request options with `userVerification: required` |
+| POST | `/passkeys/reauthenticate/verify` | `{ credential, action }` | `{ reauthToken }` |
+| POST | `/reauthenticate/password` | `{ password, action }`, authenticated session | `{ reauthToken }` |
+
+The account object has this shape (timestamps are epoch **milliseconds**):
+
+```ts
+interface Account {
+  username: string;
+  displayName: string;
+  hasPassword: boolean;
+  rpId: string;
+  userId: string; // Unpadded base64url WebAuthn user handle
+  credentials: {
+    id: string; // Unpadded base64url credential ID
+    name: string;
+    providerName?: string;
+    providerIcon?: string; // Optional base64 PNG, JPEG, or WebP data URI
+    registeredAt: number;
+    lastUsedAt?: number;
+  }[];
+}
+```
+
+Resolve provider names and icons from the credential's AAGUID on the service; unknown/zero AAGUIDs use the UI's generic key icon and provider label. AAGUIDs are display hints, never an authorization mechanism. Credential nicknames are local service labels; profile changes synchronize the account username/display name separately.
+
+## Service enforcement
+
+Use a vetted WebAuthn server library such as SimpleWebAuthn for options and verification. Persist public keys, counters, credential IDs, transports, device/backup information, AAGUID, account association, and timestamps in a durable database. Never persist biometric data or private keys.
+
+Bind short-lived, single-use challenges to the service session, ceremony type, intended action, account/user handle, and expected user-verification policy. Validate the configured RP ID, allowed UI origin, challenge, user presence, signature/counter, and expected user-verification policy on every verification. Return the configured RP ID in options/account responses; the client never guesses it from the page hostname.
+
+- **Sign-up:** `/register/options` may accept an unauthenticated username only to begin a new account registration. Reserve a unique username/user handle in the challenge session; create the account and credential atomically only after successful verification. An existing username must never let an anonymous caller add a credential to an existing account. Expire abandoned reservations. Do not trust the username repeated in the verify request.
+- **Signed-in creation:** associate the new credential with the active session account. For settings creation, require the `create` reauthentication proof on options and verification; consume it only on the final mutation. For the immediate post-password-sign-in promotion, require a recent server-recorded password authentication. Never let a client-supplied `promotion` flag bypass freshness or authorization.
+- **Creation options:** require discoverable credentials (`residentKey: required`, `requireResidentKey: true`); include existing keys in `excludeCredentials`. Set platform attachment for signup/post-login promotions; omit attachment for settings, allowing security keys. With `userVerification: preferred`, verify with `requireUserVerification: false`; otherwise match the configured requirement.
+- **Primary authentication:** use discoverable options (`allowCredentials: []`). Look up the returned credential on the service, verify it, and establish the associated account session only after successful verification. Return 404 from this verification endpoint **only** when the credential is unknown; return another status for invalid signatures/challenges. The UI uses this distinction to signal obsolete saved credentials.
+- **Reauthentication:** require an active session, return a nonempty `allowCredentials` list containing only that account's registered credentials, and require user verification. Reject assertions from another account even if their signature is valid. A password proof must verify the active account's password, never a supplied username. Issue short-lived proofs bound to the session, account, and action (`create`, `profile`, `rename:<credentialId>`, or `delete:<credentialId>`). Validate the `X-Reauthentication-Token` on every sensitive mutation, and consume it after the successful mutation. Do not authorize changes based on a client-side success flag.
+- **Management:** enforce ownership on list, rename, delete, and profile updates. Enforce username uniqueness and input length limits. Prevent deleting the final credential if no other recovery/sign-in method exists, even if the UI guard is bypassed. Never expose other accounts' credential data.
+
+Use 401 for expired/missing sessions or invalid password credentials; 403 for invalid/expired reauthentication proofs; 409 for duplicate usernames/credentials; 429 for throttling. Do not expose account/credential secrets in errors. The UI keeps verification/mutation errors visible and lets the user retry or cancel.
+
+## Browser behavior
+
+The client dynamically imports `webauthn-polyfills` after capability checks. It uses the native parse-options helpers, credential create/get methods, and `toJSON()`. Sign-in starts conditional autofill only when supported, using `autocomplete="username webauthn"`; explicit sign-in aborts the conditional request. Unmounting and canceling abort outstanding requests.
+
+Password-manager signals are feature-detected, use service-provided RP IDs and validated base64url IDs, and are nonfatal. The account list synchronizes on load and after removal. Unknown authentication credentials are signaled only on explicit verification 404s; failed registrations signal the orphaned created credential after verification failure, not after a user cancels the browser prompt.
+
+The native confirmation dialog supplies keyboard dismissal and focus containment. Removing a credential, editing its name, adding a credential in settings, and updating the profile all require fresh server verification. Password reauthentication is offered when the account has a password.
+
+## Checks
+
+- `pnpm run build`
+- `npx -y -p tsx tsx --test tests/passkeys.test.mjs` (mocked WebAuthn/API tests; no server or browser)
+- `npx -y -p typescript tsc --noEmit` (the repository currently has unrelated errors in MenuButton.tsx and utils/images.ts)
